<ContentLayout title="Desk layout basics | StackGeist" description="A practical way to lay out a gaming or work desk around usable space, reach, displays, power, and the gear you already own." active="guides">
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/?branch=guides#explore">Guides</a> / Setup basics</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Setup basics</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Build the desk around what your hands actually need to reach.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">A good-looking setup can still be annoying to use. Start with the work area, viewing position, and cable path before deciding where accessories belong.</p>

    <div class="article-summary">
      <strong>The short version</strong>
      <p>Place the chair, display, keyboard, and mouse first. Keep the center of the desk clear. Put everything else into support zones around that core.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Start here</p>
        <h2>Define the primary zone before adding anything decorative.</h2>
        <p>The primary zone is the part of the desk you touch constantly: keyboard, mouse, controller, notebook, or whatever your normal session requires. If that area is cramped, every extra object becomes friction.</p>
        <p>Clear the desk, sit in your normal position, and place only the items you use every session. Do not solve storage, lighting, speakers, charging, or decoration yet.</p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Keyboard and mouse should not force your shoulders or wrists into an awkward reach.</li>
          <li>The display should be centered on the task you do most often, not centered on the furniture for symmetry.</li>
          <li>Leave enough open surface to set down a controller, notebook, drink, or temporary device without rebuilding the desk.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Three zones</p>
        <h2>Separate primary, support, and storage gear.</h2>
        <div class="article-cards">
          <div><strong>Primary</strong><p>Things you touch constantly. Keep these closest and easiest to reset.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Support</strong><p>Speakers, charging, lighting controls, docks, and consoles. Reachable, but not in the middle.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Storage</strong><p>Headsets, spare controllers, adapters, tools, and rarely used gear. Move these off the main surface when possible.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Order matters</p>
        <h2>Use this placement order.</h2>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><strong>Chair and body position.</strong><span>Decide where you naturally sit before centering the monitor or keyboard.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Display.</strong><span>Set the main screen where you can view it without leaning or constantly turning.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Keyboard, mouse, controller.</strong><span>Protect comfortable reach and enough mouse movement for how you actually play or work.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Computer or console.</strong><span>Place it where ventilation, ports, and cleaning access make sense without stealing unnecessary desk area.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Power and cable path.</strong><span>Decide where power enters the setup before accessories multiply the cable problem.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Everything else.</strong><span>Add lighting, audio, charging, decoration, and storage only after the core layout works.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Mixed-use desks</p>
        <h2>Design for the transition between gaming and work.</h2>
        <p>If the desk handles gaming, school, or work, the best layout is usually the one that requires the fewest changes between modes. A controller that can be moved in one motion is better than a permanent arrangement that blocks writing space. A laptop dock at the edge is better than a laptop permanently occupying the center.</p>
        <div class="note">A useful test: can you switch from gaming to a normal keyboard-and-notebook task in under a minute without moving the monitor, unplugging several cables, or putting gear on the floor?</div>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Common mistakes</p>
        <h2>Things that look organized but make the desk worse.</h2>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Centering every object visually instead of centering the main task.</li>
          <li>Using the entire back edge for accessories and leaving nowhere to route cables.</li>
          <li>Buying an arm, riser, shelf, or dock before proving that the current placement is a problem.</li>
          <li>Putting frequently changed cables behind furniture where they are difficult to reach.</li>
          <li>Treating unused desk surface as something that must be filled.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="related-reading">
        <p class="page-kicker">Next</p>
        <h2>Keep improving the same setup.</h2>
        <div class="cards">
          <a class="card" href="/guides/cable-management"><h3>Cable management that stays manageable</h3><p>Route power, signal, and removable cables differently so future changes do not wreck the whole desk.</p></a>
          <a class="card" href="/guides/cheap-desk-upgrades"><h3>Cheap upgrades before expensive gear</h3><p>Fix layout, clutter, lighting, and access problems before replacing working hardware.</p></a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</ContentLayout>
